#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""b3r4 audit entry (experiment b3r4-net-and-search-audit of the package's experiments/manifest.json).

The organizer harness runs this file inside its pinned Docker sandbox, twice with
the same request, and recomputes every returned message pair with its own copy of
the selected target digest.  What it does per run:

  1. executes the *embedded, verbatim* copies of the generator (bsgen.py) and of
     the ledger (ledger.py) that produced the package, so the emitted instruction
     list and every ledger row are recomputed from source and compared with the
     constants below (gate count, operations per batch, program sha256, score);
  2. evaluates the first round of the search (256 seeded points) with the emitted
     straight-line program and compares every lane with the scalar ROUNDS-round
     reference of the same generator (the same comparison as control a);
  3. accounts the memory *as implemented*: it packs every gate into the claimed
     4-byte record (2-bit opcode + three 10-bit registers), fills and probes a real
     65,536 x 8-byte slot table with DPS_CAP entries, and recomputes the peak live
     plane count -- the sum must equal the ledger's memory row (finding F3);
  4. uses each organizer trial seed as the algorithmic coin that generates that
     trial's candidate start points, evaluates all points of a round **with the
     emitted bit-sliced program** in one 256-lane batch (not with the scalar
     reference), builds one bucket table over the low 8 digest bits, and returns
     for each trial two distinct 64-byte messages K || x, K = 0^32, whose complete
     ROUNDS-round digests agree on those bits (finding EXP-F2). The first round is
     compared lane by lane with the scalar reference and a nonzero difference
     suppresses every pair;
The program never writes to disk, never reads a path, and makes no network access.
"""
